{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","data":{"body":"Detail moved out of the description to fit the 8,000-char plan-review admission cap. No figure or decision changed; the description keeps every acceptance-bearing statement.\n\nIllustrative detail moved out of the description to stay under the 8,000-character plan-review admission cap while the three blocking findings were addressed. No figure changed.\n\nRe-measured on tickets-branch 0b9294311b5 (5,663 live tickets): 482 recorded `duplicates` links close into 148 clusters over 630 tickets, enumerating to 1,524 within-cluster pairs. eac3 measured 2,679 tickets, 43 links and 51 pairs. The growth is not uniform, and the difference matters more than the count: **1,431 of the 1,524 pairs (94%) have byte-identical normalized titles**, concentrated in 84 clusters that are entirely one repeated title — the largest is 12 copies of \"A2-1: move last_get_pass rotation bookkeeping into .bridge_state/get_rotation.json\", next is 9 copies of \"Comment triage: relocate history, rationale and contracts out of src\". Those are bulk-creation artifacts, almost certainly a retry loop, not independently authored duplicates.\n\nThis is why the benchmark cannot simply be \"all recorded pairs\". Normalized-title matching scores ~100% recall on those 1,431, so a benchmark that includes them unstratified would report near-perfect recall for the cheapest lexical candidate and reject vector search on an artifact of bulk creation — inverting the epic's decision. eac3's central finding was the opposite: the duplicates worth catching are semantic, not lexical (it measured 14% lexical recall, 44 of 51 semantic).\n\n**93 pairs have differing titles.** Those are the semantic set, and they are the benchmark: double eac3's 44, same kind, drawn from 64 mixed clusters (56 of size 2). Samples confirm the character — \"Attested snapshot fetch pulls unrelated oversized tickets branch\" vs \"Attested ref materialization broad-fetches all origin heads\"; \"rebar doctor command\" vs \"rebar doctor: diagnose-and-heal health command\".\n\n---\n\n\nFurther Context detail condensed (no figure changed):\n\nRe-measured on tickets-branch 0b9294311b5 (5,663 live tickets): 482 recorded `duplicates` links close into 148 clusters over 630 tickets, enumerating to 1,524 within-cluster pairs (eac3 measured 2,679 tickets, 43 links, 51 pairs). The difference matters more than the count: **1,431 of the 1,524 (94%) have byte-identical normalized titles**, concentrated in 84 single-title clusters — bulk-creation artifacts, almost certainly a retry loop, not independently authored duplicates. Examples are on a comment.\n\nThe benchmark therefore cannot be \"all recorded pairs\": normalized-title matching scores ~100% recall on those 1,431, so an unstratified benchmark would report near-perfect recall for the cheapest lexical candidate and reject vector search on a bulk-creation artifact — inverting the epic's decision. eac3 found the opposite (14% lexical recall, 44 of 51 semantic).\n\n**93 pairs have differing titles.** Those are the semantic set and they are the benchmark: double eac3's 44, same kind, drawn from 64 mixed clusters (56 of size 2). Sample pairs are on a comment."},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1793482125108504001,"uuid":"b330ed7e-9d18-4740-bb57-0ae3deb9028b"}